WEBVTT

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:04.540
We're going to be talking about Judaism and
technology and the dance between the two,

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:11.620
and this idea of the renewing the
old and making the new sacred.

00:00:12.290 --> 00:00:19.170
Technology helps us look at all of our
tradition that is ancient in new ways,

00:00:19.210 --> 00:00:22.920
and Judaism helps us think about
technology and make it sacred,

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.390
to use in our lives in ways
beyond just a work or hobby.

00:00:27.430 --> 00:00:32.160
And this dance of Judaism
technology is as old as time itself.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.180
It starts at the very beginning.

00:00:36.310 --> 00:00:38.450
And in the beginning, God created light.

00:00:39.170 --> 00:00:43.520
And God saw that the light was good,
and God separated the light from darkness.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:50.120
A few days later, God created human beings,
b'tzelem Elohim, in the image of God.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.930
And God looked at everything
and said, this is very good.

00:00:54.710 --> 00:01:01.450
And ever since then, we have been using our
divine gifts, our God-like gift to create,

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:07.480
to shape our world. We create art, we create
music, we create stories, we create tools.

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.830
And some of these creations
have transformed our reality.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:16.030
With every new technology that
we encounter, especially as Jews,

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:21.200
there are some clear benefits to them,
and there are some drawbacks.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:26.550
And we sort of wrestle as a people when a
particular technology has more clear benefits than

00:01:26.590 --> 00:01:32.100
drawbacks, we adopt it, and we bring it into
what we do as a people and as a religion.

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:36.010
But it's this sort of two-steps-forward,

00:01:36.050 --> 00:01:39.740
one-step-back kind of dance that
we do with technology as we go.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:45.940
So one of our oldest technologies I
like to call our sacred text containers.

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:50.660
And this is really unique to Judaism
because we are People of the Book.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.460
That is what we're called. But the
reality is a book is a new technology.

00:01:55.500 --> 00:02:02.580
And then along came screens. And screens really
are perhaps even more of a game-changer than the

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:11.090
printing press was. Because this little piece of
glass and plastic and silicon in my pocket has

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:15.730
access to, essentially, the entirety
of human knowledge and information.

00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:19.820
That's wild. This is a very new development.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.950
And it necessarily impacts
Jewish life and Jewish living.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:28.200
We now have access to all of the
texts of our tradition in our pockets.

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:32.230
And it's never been like that in the history
of Judaism, in the history of humanity.

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:35.270
But we are hard-pressed to let
go of technology that we love.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:38.150
We still read from the Torah scroll every week.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.220
It is not the most efficient
way to access the text.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:45.250
But it is meaningful to us,
and it connects us to our past.

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:46.810
And so we hold on to it.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:49.450
So it's this dance. We go back and forth.

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:53.620
We adopt new things. We hold
on to other things as we go.

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:57.640
and instead of screens, it's AI,
but it's the same conversation.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:03.560
It's the same idea of how
do we make our tradition,

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.760
how do we renew our tradition using these tools,

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:09.740
and how do we use these tools in a way that
makes them holy and sacred and special?

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.100
But we are Yisrael.
We are a people who struggle.

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:23.350
That is what our name means. And so it is arguably
our obligation to wrestle with this latest

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:26.680
technology, AI, and what it means for us.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:32.690
So, in some ways, this technology,
in particular AI, is a mirror for us.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:38.090
While we are created in God's image,
we are creating AI in our image.

00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:43.140
It is being trained on the totality of
human work and human writing and human art.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:49.170
And so, it is becoming like us, in a way.

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:55.260
And what we put into it is what
we're going to see come out of it.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:04:00.240
But this is our job, and I would argue our
obligation as Jews when we experience new

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.460
technologies. We have to engage with it.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:06.170
We have to understand it and
know what it is and what it does.

00:04:06.210 --> 00:04:10.000
We have to understand the benefits and the
drawbacks so we can make an informed choice about

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:13.110
whether or how we adopt it into our practice.

00:04:13.150 --> 00:04:18.630
These are just some of the values rooted deeply
in Jewish tradition that I think we need to

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:24.340
bring to AI and our work with AI, and that
is honesty, emet, compassion, rachamim,

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.380
humility, anavah, and justice, tzedek.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:31.850
And if we can build these
into AI and our use of AI,

00:04:32.840 --> 00:04:38.150
its impact on the world as a whole will be far
greater than it currently seems like it's going.

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:43.500
But this weekend, this is what,
this is our task for the weekend,

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.220
is figuring out how AI can fit
into Jewish life and practice,

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:54.440
into our own personal practice, how we can
sanctify this new technology and make our lives a

00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:00.840
little bit more holy and gain new insight
and refresh some of our ancient tradition.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:06.820
And this is a quote that Rav Abraham
Isaac Kook said: Hayashan yitchadeish,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:10.430
v'hechadash yitkadeish. Sounds much
better in the Hebrew than in English.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:12.240
But that's what we'll do.

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:13.920
Thank you again for having me.

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:14.720
Shabbat shalom.

